1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,670
This is VayuVyuha, our working prototype for dynamic air operations planning.

2
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:07,398
Everything you will see runs on synthetic data.

3
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:09,310
This is the Command view.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,662
The planner has built a twelve hour plan.

5
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:18,326
Eighteen of twenty seven requested missions are tasked, and every aircraft, crew, weapon and runway limit is respected.

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,638
Click any mission to see its package.

7
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:25,246
It shows which aircraft fly it, what they carry, and why this package was chosen.

8
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:29,422
Some requests cannot be met with today's resources.

9
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:37,134
For each one, the planner names the limit that blocks it, such as weapon stock, crew hours, or aircraft that are already committed.

10
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:39,726
Now let us change the situation.

11
00:00:40,070 --> 00:00:44,422
One and a half hours in, a jet fails its inspection, so we remove it from the pool.

12
00:00:44,562 --> 00:00:47,778
The planner re-plans in a fraction of a second, and then waits.

13
00:00:47,918 --> 00:00:50,446
The banner says what happened and what it recommends.

14
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,014
The list shows each change and the reason for it.

15
00:00:53,154 --> 00:00:55,378
Missions that have already launched are never touched.

16
00:00:55,518 --> 00:00:56,158
We approve.

17
00:00:56,430 --> 00:00:59,294
Next, a new air defence site appears on a planned route.

18
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:00,622
We place it on the map.

19
00:01:00,762 --> 00:01:04,730
The planner recomputes the risk, and adds an escort where one is needed.

20
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:06,814
Let us compare the options.

21
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:11,278
Recommended, cautious, and maximum coverage are all valid plans.

22
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:14,990
They differ in how much risk and change the commander accepts.

23
00:01:15,510 --> 00:01:18,758
A manual style re-plan is shown only for reference.

24
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:20,726
We approve the recommended plan.

25
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:24,886
An urgent target appears, with a seventy five minute window.

26
00:01:25,030 --> 00:01:26,054
We place it on the map.

27
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:29,618
The planner fits it in, and shows which lower value mission gives way.

28
00:01:29,758 --> 00:01:32,638
We open the new mission to see its package, and then approve.

29
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:36,758
Finally, a storm closes a strike corridor.

30
00:01:36,910 --> 00:01:38,414
We place it on the map as well.

31
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:41,934
Missions inside it are moved or dropped, and again we approve.

32
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:46,358
The Timeline view shows every sortie, aircraft by aircraft.

33
00:01:46,498 --> 00:01:48,338
Shaded bars have already launched.

34
00:01:48,478 --> 00:01:50,334
Hatched rows are grounded aircraft.

35
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:55,310
The Fleet view shows each base.

36
00:01:55,450 --> 00:02:00,842
We see weapon stock, crew hours used, and the predicted chance that an aircraft fails its pre-flight check.

37
00:02:00,982 --> 00:02:04,470
Unreliable aircraft are kept off the highest priority missions.

38
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:06,706
The Engine view shows what happens inside.

39
00:02:06,846 --> 00:02:09,022
Nine rule checks run on every plan.

40
00:02:09,162 --> 00:02:17,370
We see how the plan was found, what the score is made of, and how much mission value survives across one thousand simulated days of aircraft failures.

41
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:20,918
We can also explore the trade-off between risk and coverage.

42
00:02:21,058 --> 00:02:23,458
Each dot is a complete, valid plan.

43
00:02:23,598 --> 00:02:26,558
So far, the exact solver was running on a server.

44
00:02:26,698 --> 00:02:29,290
The same app also works with no server at all.

45
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:33,798
We switch to the built-in engine, which plans inside the browser, and ground another jet.

46
00:02:33,938 --> 00:02:36,258
It re-plans just as quickly, and we approve.

47
00:02:37,670 --> 00:02:38,630
VayuVyuha.

48
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:44,622
One live picture, a new plan in under a second, a reason for every change, and a human who approves.

49
00:02:44,910 --> 00:02:45,438
Thank you.
